2026-06-30 15:13:53,208 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-6a4e40e7-fe7e-4601-9457-4a6a8624571d', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20191223_01.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20191223_02.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20191223_03.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20191223_05.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20191223_06.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200215_01.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200215_02.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200215_04.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200215_05.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200215_07.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200215_08.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200216_01.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200216_02.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200216_04.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200216_05.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200216_07.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200216_08.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200217_01.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200217_02.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200217_04.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200217_05.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200217_07.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200217_08.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200403_06.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200403_07.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200403_08.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200403_09.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200404_01.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200404_02.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200404_03.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200404_04.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200404_06.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200404_07.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200404_08.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200404_09.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200528_01.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200528_02.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200528_03.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200528_05.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200528_07.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200528_08.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200528_09.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200528_11.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200528_13.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200528_14.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200528_15.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200528_17.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200718_01.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200718_02.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200718_03.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200718_04.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200718_07.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200718_08.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200718_09.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200718_10.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200719_01.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200719_02.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200719_03.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200719_04.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200719_07.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200719_08.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200719_09.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200719_10.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200720_01.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200720_02.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200720_03.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200720_04.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200720_06.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200720_07.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200730_01.raw\nOrbitrapLUMOS_20200730_02.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nEnabling Photoactivated Cross-Linking Mass Spectrometric Analysis of Protein Complexes by Novel MS-Cleavable Cross-Linkers\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nCross-linking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) is a powerful tool for studying protein–protein interactions and elucidating architectures of protein complexes. While residue-specific XL-MS studies have been very successful, accessibility of interaction regions nontargetable by specific chemistries remain difficult. Photochemistry has shown great potential in capturing those regions because of nonspecific reactivity, but low yields and high complexities of photocross-linked products have hindered their identification, limiting current studies predominantly to single proteins. Here, we describe the development of three novel MS-cleavable heterobifunctional cross-linkers, namely SDASO (Succinimidyl diazirine sulfoxide), to enable fast and accurate identification of photocross-linked peptides by MSn. The MSn-based workflow allowed SDASO XL-MS analysis of the yeast 26S proteasome, demonstrating the feasibility of photocross-linking of large protein complexes for the first time. Comparative analyses have revealed that SDASO cross-linking is robust and captures interactions complementary to residue-specific reagents, providing the foundation for future applications of photocross-linking in complex XL-MS studies.\nGraphical Abstract\nHighlights\nDevelopment of three mass spectrometry-cleavable photoreactive cross-linkers.\nCharacterization of diazirine-based photocross-linking with a standard protein BSA.\nMSn-based workflow for photocross-linking analysis of protein complexes.\nSDASO cross-linking mass spectrometry analyses of the yeast 26S proteasome complex.\nIn Brief\nAlthough photochemistry complements residue-specific chemistry through labeling amino acids nonspecifically, existing photo-cross-linking reagents are thus far inapplicable to multisubunit protein complexes owing to low yields and high complexities of photo-cross-linked products. The development of the three sulfoxide-containing MS-cleavable photoreactive SDASO cross-linkers permits MSn-based analytical workflow for accurate identification of photo-cross-linked peptides, enabling complex PPI profiling for the first time. This work has established a solid foundation for future applications of photo-cross-linking in complex XL-MS studies.\n\n[METHODS]\nExperimental Procedures\nSynthesis and Characterization of SDASO Cross-Linkers\nThree SDASO cross-linkers were designed, synthesized, and analyzed in this work (Fig.\xa01), including SDASO-L, SDASO-M, and SDASO-S. Their synthesis and characterization are described in supplemental Fig.\xa0S1 and supplemental Methods.\nPurification of the Yeast 26S Proteasome Complexes\nRPN11-TAP strain was used for yeast proteasome purification as previously described. Briefly, the yeast strain was cultured in YEPD medium (1% yeast extract, 2% peptone, and 2% dextrose) at 30 °C until stationary phase, then the cells were collected and washed with ice-cold water. The yeast cells were loaded into 20\xa0ml syringe and pushed into liquid nitrogen to get yeast frozen “noodles” which were ground in a Cryomill into frozen powder. The yeast frozen powder was resuspended in a lysis buffer containing 50\xa0mM sodium phosphate (pH 8.0), 5\xa0mM magnesium chloride, 1\xa0mM ATP, 1×\xa0protease inhibitor (Sigma), then sonicated at 15\xa0W with 30 s on and 30 s off for three cycles, and pellet was spun down at 15,000\xa0rpm for 15\xa0min. The supernatant was bound to IgG resin (MP Biomedical#55961) for 2\xa0h at 4 °C with rotation. The IgG resin was washed with 50 bed volume of wash buffer (50\xa0mM sodium phosphate [pH 7.4], 100\xa0mM NaCl, 5\xa0mM magnesium chloride, 1\xa0mM ATP), then 20 bed volume of TEV cleavage buffer (50\xa0mM Sodium phosphate [pH 7.4], 10% glycerol, 1\xa0mM EDTA, 1\xa0mM DTT and 1\xa0mM ATP), and the bound proteasome was cleaved overnight with TEV at 4 °C. The resulting proteasome was concentrated with 30K cutoff Centricon devices (EMD Millipore UFC903024).\nXL-MS Analysis of BSA and 26S Proteasome\nProtein cross-linking was performed similarly to previous studies with some modifications. Briefly, for SDASO cross-linking of BSA, 50\xa0μl of 50\xa0μM protein solution in PBS buffer (pH 7.4) was reacted in triplicate with SDASO-L, SDASO-M, or SDASO-S in molar ratio of 1:50, respectively, for 1\xa0h at 25 °C in the dark. The NHS reactive ends were quenched with the addition of ammonium bicarbonate at a 50-fold excess for 10\xa0min at 25 °C in the dark. Then NHS ester labeled proteins were transferred into Millipore Microcon Ultracel PL-30 (30-kDa filters) and washed three times with 300\xa0μl PBS buffer. Diazirine cross-linking was activated by UV irradiation, which was carried out on ice ~5\xa0cm from the light source in an UV light chamber (Analytikjena UVP Cross-linker CL-1000L) and irradiated at 365\xa0nm for 30\xa0min.\nThe affinity purified yeast 26S proteasome was (supplemental Methods) cross-linked by SDASO linkers similarly as described above. To determine the optimal SDASO cross-linking conditions, we have performed initial XL-MS experiments of the yeast 26S proteasome using 5, 10, 20, and 40\xa0mM SDASO, respectively. As a result, 20\xa0mM SDASO yielded the highest number of cross-link identifications and was determined as the optimal cross-linking condition for this work. Specifically, 100\xa0μg of the 26S proteasome in PBS buffer (pH 7.4) was cross-linked in triplicate with 20\xa0mM SDASO-L, SDASO-M, and SDASO-S, respectively. In addition, 100\xa0μg of the yeast 26S proteasome in PBS buffer (pH 7.4) was cross-linked with 2.5\xa0mM or 5\xa0mM DSSO for 1\xa0h at 25 °C temp similarly as described, and the reactions were quenched with the addition of ammonium bicarbonate at a 50-fold excess for 10\xa0min. Then cross-linked proteins were transferred into Millipore Microcon Ultracel PL-30 (30-kDa filters) for digestion.\nDigestion of Cross-Linked Proteins\nThe resulting cross-linked products were subjected to enzymatic digestion using a FASP protocol. Briefly, cross-linked proteins on FASP filters were reduced/alkylated and digested with Lys-C/trypsin or chymotrypsin as described. The resulting digests were desalted, and cross-linked peptides were enriched by size-exclusion chromatography before LC MSn analysis.\nExperimental Design and Statistical Rationale\nThree SDASO cross-linkers were designed and characterized in this work with a standard protein BSA and an affinity purified yeast proteasome. Each SDASO XL-MS experiment was performed in biological triplicate under optimized conditions. To evaluate the effect of enzymatic digestion on SDASO results, chrymotrypsin digestion was performed for SDASO-L cross-linked 26S proteasome in biological triplicate as well. In total, nine SDASO XL-MS experiments for BSA analysis and 12 SDASO XL-MS experiments for the yeast 26S proteasome. Cross-validation was carried out among the results obtained from the three SDASO linkers. To further evaluate the SDASO results, DSSO XL-MS experiments of the yeast 26S proteasome were performed in two biological replicates. Reproducibility in XL-MS experiments was assessed at the level of cross-linked peptide sequences and sites, respectively.\nLC-MSn Analysis and Identification of Cross-Linked Peptides\nCross-linked peptides were analyzed by LC-MSn using a Thermo Scientific Dionex UltiMate 3000 system online coupled with an Orbitrap Fusion Lumos mass spectrometer. A 50\xa0cm\xa0× 75\xa0μm Acclaim PepMap C18 column was used to separate peptides over a gradient of 1% to 25% ACN in 106\xa0min for BSA and in 166\xa0min for the 26S proteasome at a flow rate of 300\xa0nl/min. MS1 scans (375–1500\xa0m/z, resolution at 120,000) were performed with the AGC target set to 4e5 in top speed mode with a cycle time of 5\xa0s. For MSn analysis, 3+ and up charged ions were selected for MS2-CID in FT mode, followed by top four data-dependent MS3 acquisition method. A targeted MS3 acquisition was also used for DSSO cross-linked peptides by utilizing the mass difference between alkene- and thiol-modified ion pairs (31.9721\xa0Da). For MS2 scans, the resolution was set to 30,000, the AGC target 5e4, the precursor isolation width was 1.6\xa0m/z, and the maximum injection time was 100\xa0ms for CID. The CID-MS2 normalized collision energy was 25%. For MS3 scans, CID was used with a collision energy of 35%, the AGC target was set to 2e4, and the maximum injection time was set to 120\xa0ms.\nIdentification of Cross-Linked Peptides\nMSn data were extracted using MSConvert (ProteoWizard 3.0.10738) and analyzed similarly as previously described. Briefly, the extracted MS3 data were subjected to a developmental version of Protein Prospector (v.6.0.0) for database searching, using Batch-Tag against a custom random concatenated database (a total of 988 entries) derived from BSA and 493 Saccharomyces cerevisiae protein sequences that were identified from the affinity purified yeast 26S proteasomes. The mass tolerances for parent ions and fragment ions set as ±20\xa0ppm and 0.6\xa0Da, respectively. Trypsin or chymotrypsin was set as the enzyme with three or four maximum missed cleavages allowed, respectively. A maximum of four variable modifications were allowed, including cysteine carbamidomethylation, protein N-terminal acetylation, methionine oxidation, and N-terminal conversion of glutamine to pyroglutamic acid. In addition, three defined modifications representing alkene on uncleaved lysines, thiol and sulfenic fragment moieties on any amino acid (AAs) were selected for each respective SDASO cross-linker. Specifically, for SDASO-L cross-links: alkene (C3H2O;\xa0+54\xa0Da), sulfenic acid (C7H13NO2S;\xa0+175\xa0Da), and thiol (C7H11NOS;\xa0+157\xa0Da). For SDASO-M cross-links: alkene (C3H2O;\xa0+54\xa0Da), sulfenic acid (C5H10OS;\xa0+118\xa0Da), and thiol (C5H8S;\xa0+100\xa0Da). For SDASO-S cross-links: alkene (C3H2O;\xa0+54\xa0Da), sulfenic acid (C4H8OS;\xa0+104\xa0Da), and thiol (C4H6S;\xa0+86\xa0Da). For DSSO cross-links, three defined modifications on uncleaved lysines are: alkene (C3H2O;\xa0+54\xa0Da), sulfenic acid (C3H4O2S;\xa0+104\xa0Da), and thiol (C3H2SO;\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nSaccharomyces cerevisiae (baker\'s yeast); Bos taurus (bovine)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
